                "text": "**Debugging compilation errors**\n\nIt looks like the \"property value expected\" compile error might happen due to a missing semicolon or some extra characters. The line shows \"color: @on_primary;\" which seems okay, but I wonder if the multi-select of selectors spanning across lines is the issue. \n\nThe structure looks valid, yet maybe the parser doesn't support the @ notation for variables? I should consider using var(--on_primary) instead, since earlier uses of @variables have worked without any errors. The problem could also stem from @on_primary not being defined in this file but maybe in colors.css with the proper '--on-primary' definition.**Examining CSS compilation issues**\n\nThe import of \"colors.css\" includes @define-color statements, and I wonder if this syntax is specific to swaync, based on GTK's CSS. It’s curious that the variable name @on_primary has an underscore, while CSS may prefer hyphenated names. Other variables with underscores like @primary_container have been used before, which is interesting. \n\nThe compile error \"property value expected\" might indicate that the parser is misinterpreting the \"@\" symbol as an at-rule. Since they did define colors earlier using GTK syntax, that should be valid. I’m also considering if a missing semicolon after \"transition: all 0.2s ease-in-out;\" could be the culprit, especially since the error points to line 125 in a file with 407 lines.**Finding a solution for color variable issue**\n\nI can’t easily count lines right now, but the user pointed out that selection from line 125, columns 10 to 21 is the color line. I’m thinking of changing \"color: @on_primary;\" to \"color: @on_primary !important;\", but that likely won’t fix the \"property value expected\" error.\n\nA safer fix would be to replace @on_primary with an actual color value like rgb(...), 'inherit', or 'white'. Maybe I should use \"color: @on_primary; /* fallback */\" instead. Still, we want to minimize edits for consistency since @on_primary appears in many places, even if only one line is flagged. It’s curious why this line is singled out. Perhaps the parser is expecting a curly bracket on the same line because it’s inside multiple selectors.**Proposing a fix for CSS issue**\n\nI think a potential fix is to change \"color: @on_primary;\" to \"color: @on_primary !important;\", but I'm doubtful that will work. I could also rewrite it as \"color: @on_primary; /* color variable */\" without changing anything. \n\nHmm, maybe the issue arises from stray invisible characters in the code causing the parser error, which we can’t easily spot. I’m proposing we replace the whole block with the same text but ensure it's clean of any potential Unicode characters. \n\nSo, I might suggest replacing @on_primary with @on_primary_color to see if that helps. Following the developer's instructions, I’ll summarize with a concise solution and code modifications.**Proposing a color variable replacement**\n\nI think a good solution would be to replace the problematic color variable with a valid CSS color, possibly using @on_primary and alpha(@primary, 0.8). However, I might just change it to @on_surface, which is also safe and valid. \n\nI’ll outline the modification: we need to replace \"color: @on_primary;\" with \"color: @on_surface;\". This ensures that the parser recognizes @on_surface exists. I’ll provide the original lines in the FIND block, maintaining all indentation and comments, followed by the REPLACE block with the updated color. \n\nFinally, I’ll include a one-sentence description: \"Replace the problematic color variable with a valid defined color (e.g., @on_surface) to satisfy the parser.\" I’ll wrap it all up with ---COMPLETE. Let’s finalize this replacement!",
